 1 big thing: AI backlash hits ballot box in key races 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Democrats are seeking to weaponize AI in four competitive House races, launching a $4 million ad campaign to attack GOP incumbents based on new polling data shared exclusively with Axios.

 Why it matters: The midterms have been called the first AI election , and the new campaign is one of a few key tests of how deeply voter anxiety over the revolutionary technology will sway outcomes at the ballot box.

 Driving the news: The Democratic group Tomorrow Together plans to run ads calling out Republicans in swing districts in Wisconsin, Arizona, Pennsylvania and Michigan for a 2025 vote they cast to block state AI regulations.

 The attack is based on new polls showing that voters are more concerned about efforts to block state AI regulation than they are about data center controversies. The data points to a shift in voter sentiment around AI. While previous polls showed that Americans were unsure which political party to trust, surveys of the four districts showed a significant advantage on the issue for Democrats. Voters trust Democrats over Republicans to hold AI executives accountable by a margin of seven to 10 points across the four swing districts, according to Workbench Strategy, which Tomorrow Together commissioned to do the surveys. The Wisconsin, Arizona, Pennsylvania and Michigan districts are all in presidential battlegrounds and will be a bellwether for AI issues in the midterms. Catch up quick: The House in May 2025 passed a budget bill that included a 10-year moratorium on state-level AI regulation.

 Republican Reps. Derrick Van Orden (Wis.), Juan Ciscomani (Ariz.), Rob Bresnahan (Pa.) and Tom Barrett (Mich.) all voted in favor of the budget bill that included the moratorium. Widespread, bipartisan backlash quickly erupted, and on July 1 the Senate voted 99-1 to strip the moratorium from the package, including its chief sponsor, Sen. Ted Cruz (R-Texas). The big picture: Democrats want to make AI a winning issue for them in elections.

 The Workbench Strategy data points to widespread voter skepticism of AI companies, with majorities in all four districts saying candidates' AI positions matter to their vote. The poll also found broad support for AI accountability measures, including making data centers cover utility costs, holding AI companies liable for harm and protecting consumers. The bottom line: Preemption, once considered a wonky, niche policy issue, is emerging as a core theme in battleground state attack ads, an indicator of just how much AI issues may swing the upcoming election.

 
 
