 2. AI may not be boosting productivity much yet 
 
 By Courtenay Brown 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco ; Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios America is in the midst of its strongest productivity surge in decades. The evidence is more ambiguous about how much AI is the reason. 

 Why it matters: Companies are pouring hundreds of billions of dollars into AI, a massive bet that the technology will deliver sweeping productivity gains across the economy.

 But so far, the productivity gains may have more to do with less sexy developments, including the more intensive use of capital equipment and a post-pandemic surge in entrepreneurship now paying dividends. What they're saying: "Translating individual productivity to organizational productivity — there's a lot of slip between cup and lip," Keith Waddell, CEO of staffing firm Robert Half, told investors last week.

 "We're not ignoring that there are productivity gains for our people, and we're getting some," he added. S&amp;P Global aims for roughly 2% in annual productivity gains, but it cannot isolate AI's contribution from other operational changes.

 "Some of that is directly AI. Some of that is non-AI. Most of it is actually mixed and integrated," CFO Eric Aboaf said Tuesday on the firm's earnings call. The other side: The productivity gains are more apparent among companies developing AI.

 Anthropic disclosed in its IPO filing this week that Claude wrote more than 90% of contributions to its codebase last month. The average daily volume of code contributed per engineer was more than 45 times its 2024 level, according to the prospectus. What to watch: Whether AI's benefits spread beyond tech giants and show up in broader corporate financial results.

